From: Dimitry B <dimitryB@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 00:22:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11/18] eip155: one settlement per authorization; answer a
 pending one's retry from its transaction
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

x402 v2 §9 makes settlement_pending non-terminal: the caller reconciles and may
send the same settle request again. The TS SDK's resource server does so once,
immediately (settleWithPendingRetry). x402-rs kept no record of the first
attempt, so the retry was settled from scratch: a second broadcast while the
first transaction was still pending, or invalid_transaction_state once it had
spent the nonce, although the payment had gone through. Meanwhile /verify still
called the authorization valid while its transaction was unmined, so a seller
could serve another request with the same payment.

The v2 exact handler now keeps one record per payment authorization (chain,
payer, EIP-3009 or Permit2 nonce) from the start of a settle until its outcome
is known:

- While a record exists, /verify of that authorization, and /settle of any
  other request carrying it, get invalid_transaction_state (new
  PaymentVerificationError::AuthorizationInFlight): the authorization is
  already committed to a settlement and must not pay for anything else.
- A settle that ends in anything but settlement_pending drops its record when
  it returns (a drop guard, so also if the request is cancelled).
- A retry of the very same request (same parsed payload, signature and
  requirements) sends nothing and is answered from the transaction's receipt:
  settlement_pending with the same hash while unmined; success with the hash
  for the first retry that sees it mined, which removes the record atomically,
  so concurrent retries cannot both get the success and every later settle is
  an ordinary replay (invalid_transaction_state); a reverted transaction
  removes the record and gets invalid_transaction_state with its hash.
- Records are in memory, bounded (10,000, oldest dropped first) and expire
  after 15 minutes.

A settlement transaction that was mined but reverted is now reported with its
hash in transaction (new X402SchemeFacilitatorError::TransactionFailed, x402 v2
§9 invalid_transaction_state, HTTP 400) instead of unexpected_error with
"transaction": "": §5.3.2 allows "" only if nothing was broadcast. Such a
revert is no longer re-explained as a replay.
